+# Daily Grind security audit and deployment
+
+## Scope and findings
+
+The repository contains one static page. The audit covered its HTML, CSS resource
+loads, package scripts, and deployment configuration. There are no client scripts,
+DOM injection sinks, event handlers, cookies, forms, storage, API calls, popups,
+iframes, analytics, or third-party executable dependencies. No security headers
+were configured in this repository before this change. Live response headers were
+not inspected; hosting infrastructure may add its own headers.
+
+The inline stylesheet and heading style were moved to `assets/styles.css` without
+changing the design. This allows a policy without `unsafe-inline`. The hero image
+still uses the existing HTTPS Unsplash URL; only that origin is allowed for images.
+It is a mutable image, not executable code, so no fragile integrity hash is attached.
+Document and asset referrer policies strip paths and queries on cross-origin loads.
+
+The default CSP denies all resource types unless explicitly needed, blocks all
+scripts and inline event handlers, denies plugins and base URL overrides, restricts
+form submissions to this origin, and disables framing. Trusted Types enforcement
+and `trusted-types 'none'` prevent future code from creating bypass policies or
+writing strings to injection sinks in supporting browsers. No JavaScript or
+Trusted Types polyfill is needed: the page has no runtime behavior. Browsers without
+Trusted Types still benefit from the script prohibition and safe static markup.
+
+The `/account`, `/logout`, order, and other navigation URLs are links only; this
+repository contains no implementations. Authentication, authorization, session
+cookies, CSRF protection, Fetch Metadata checks, and logout data clearing must be
+handled by the backend that owns those routes. In particular, a GET to `/logout`
+should not mutate session state; use a confirmation page and CSRF-protected POST
+when implementing that backend.
+
+## Static deployment
+
+Run `npm run build` and publish **only `dist/`**. No server is started. Deploy to
+a static host supporting Netlify/Cloudflare Pages `_headers` syntax, or translate
+`dist/_headers` to equivalent HTTP response headers in your host/CDN configuration.
+The `/*` rule covers pages, styles, and other static responses. Dynamic/proxied
+responses may require separate header configuration at their origin. The existing
+`start` script is only a local static server and is not relied on for headers.
+
+The HTML includes a CSP fallback before resource loads for hosts that ignore
+`_headers`. It cannot provide framing protection, HSTS, Permissions Policy, COOP,
+CORP, MIME protection, or reporting. Those require HTTP response headers. Do not
+consider deployment complete until your hosting configuration applies them.
+
+Serve over HTTPS, redirect HTTP to HTTPS, and use correct MIME types (HTML as
+`text/html`, CSS as `text/css`). HSTS begins at 300 seconds to make rollback
+practical. Increase its lifetime only after HTTPS coverage is established. Do not
+add `includeSubDomains` or preload without auditing all subdomains.
+
+COOP uses `same-origin-allow-popups` to preserve outbound OAuth/payment popup
+compatibility if later added. CORP defaults to `same-origin`; adjust specific asset
+routes only if deliberate cross-origin embedding is required. Unused camera,
+microphone, geolocation, payment, and USB APIs are disabled. COEP and cross-origin
+isolation are omitted because the page does not need shared memory, and they can
+break external resources. No permissive CORS header is added.
+
+## Policy changes and reporting
+
+The current script-free page has an enforced policy by default after a complete
+source inventory. When introducing scripts, vendors, embeds, or changing policies,
+first deploy a candidate using:
+
+```sh
+SECURITY_POLICY_MODE=report-only SECURITY_REPORT_URL=https://your-collector.example/csp npm run build
+```
+
+Replace the example URL with a real HTTPS collector you operate or trust. This
+repository does not implement a collector. It must accept browser POSTs in both
+Reporting API (`application/reports+json`) and legacy CSP (`application/csp-report`)
+formats. The build rejects credentials, query strings, fragments, and header
+delimiters in this URL. Without a collector setting, no endpoint is advertised;
+violations are available in browser diagnostics only, with no fleet telemetry.
+
+Report-only builds remove the enforcing HTML CSP and retain only enforced framing
+protection alongside the candidate `Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only` header.
+Other companion headers remain enforced. Report-only does **not** prevent XSS;
+limit that deployment to the intended rollout window. Use a host honoring HTTP
+headers: HTML meta cannot express a report-only policy.
+
+Collect representative traffic over days or weeks, separate extension/browser
+noise from real resource violations, and resolve legitimate failures before
+promotion. Treat reports as untrusted input: rate-limit and size-limit the collector,
+redact URL queries/fragments, credentials and personal data before logging, limit
+retention/access, and never render report fields as HTML. Code samples are not
+requested by this script-free policy to avoid collecting user content.
+
+Promote with `SECURITY_POLICY_MODE=enforce` and the same `SECURITY_REPORT_URL` to
+retain reporting. Keep a previous deployment for rollback; narrowing HSTS after
+deployment does not immediately clear existing browser caches. If scripts become
+necessary, authorize reviewed static bytes with hashes (and SRI for external
+scripts) rather than broad host allowlists or a fixed nonce. Refactor any DOM sinks
+before changing Trusted Types, and keep text insertion on `textContent`.
+
+## Verification
+
+`npm test` checks the current resource contract, CSP/header agreement, absence of
+inline execution, enforcement/report-only output, reporting configuration, and
+rejection of invalid rollout inputs. It only runs static build scripts and leaves
+`dist/` in default enforced mode. No browser verification or dev/preview server is
+used. Hosting header delivery and real traffic compatibility remain deployment
+checks; source checks cannot prove that a CDN applies these headers.
+
+References: [MDN CSP guide](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/CSP)
+and [CSP report-only header](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only).
